# Spotted Lantern Flytrap — Client Proposal (MAE 2250)
team: Batties  
clients: Cornell CALS Extension, E&J Gallo Winery, and National Grape

**Spotted Lanternflies (SLF)** can heavily infest vineyards—up to **400 SLF per vine**—and repeated feeding can contribute to as much as **90% yield loss** over time (Cornell CALS, n.d.; Pfeiffer et al., 2022). In this proposal, we focus on a trap-based solution to reduce SLF pressure in vineyard environments.

---

## Problem Statement
Up to 400 SLF can feed on each vine in a vineyard (Cornell CALS, n.d.). Over time, repetitive feeding can cause up to 90% yield loss (Pfeiffer et al., 2022).

---

## Impact
As long as SLF continue to feed on grape vines, grapes are less likely to survive to harvest. Narrowing our focus to **defending vineyards** allows us to create an industry-specific solution.

---

## Proposed Direction

### Concept: Spotted Lantern Flytrap

**What it is (attract with bait + capture):**  
We are exploring:
- **Sticky surface capture** (insects stick to an adhesive)
- **Low-power fan containment** (fan pulls insects into a collection chamber)
- **Soap-water capture** (use of a liquid reservoir)

**How it would be used:**
- Hang traps on trees
- Check and clear out traps periodically

**Why it’s better than the status quo:**
- There are too many SLF to try and kill by hand
- Less chance of contamination later on

**End-of-semester proof-of-concept:**  
Build and test **one** trap module: a **single one-way entrance chamber** with bait inside to evaluate whether SLF can be reliably attracted and captured using **passive bait + mechanical containment**.

---

## Key Risks / Unknowns
- **Capturing pollinators:** Many are already endangered. We will test our bait outside to see what else is attracted.
- **SLF ignoring the bait:** If they prefer grapes, traps won’t work. We will place various baits (including grape vines) in front of SLF and observe which they choose.
- **SLF escaping the trap:** This negates our goal. We will test with SLF and SLF-sized objects to identify escape routes and seal failure modes.

---

## Questions for the Client
1. **Are there substances more attractive to SLF than grape vines, and are they attracted to scent, sight, or something else?**  
   *Decision affected:* choosing the best attraction mechanism for the flytrap.

2. **Which other insects are attracted to the same items as SLF?**  
   *Decision affected:* bait selection while minimizing non-target capture.

3. **What is the size/weight of SLF when they enter vineyards?**  
   *Decision affected:* entrance geometry + net/mesh sizing.

